"## Grouping page: The Middle Horizon (Andes)\n\nTapestry tunics, a staff-holding god and knotted cords spread across the Andes about 600–1000 CE; whether an empire spread them is argued.\n\n**Card**\n\n| Part | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Kind | Horizon (HOR002), and the period of the same name (PRD189). It is an archaeologists' time term, not a people or a state. It opens onto two polities, Wari (POL058) and Tiwanaku (POL059), and one shared style (STY065). |\n| Defined by (who, when) | John H. Rowe's Andean chronology of the 1960s, which alternates \"horizons\" of wide-spread style with \"intermediate periods\" (canon HOR001–HOR003). Dorothy Menzel's 1964 monograph split it into ceramic epochs (Middle Horizon 1–4, with sub-phases such as 1A and 1B). Ann Pollard Rowe (2024) re-dates the ceramics Menzel gave to Middle Horizon 3, so the end of the horizon is itself being redrawn. Luis Lumbreras's rival scheme (PRD192) calls the same centuries the \"Wari Empire\" stage. |\n| Span | About 600–1000 CE, the conventional range (Jennings 2006; Conlee and others 2021; the Met). Tiwanaku starts earlier: UNESCO gives 400–900 CE for its flourishing and a collapse in the first half of the 12th century. |\n",
"| Places | Wari: the capital near Ayacucho, Peru; Conchopata nearby; Pikillacta near Cusco; Cerro Baúl in Moquegua; Huaca del Loro in Nasca; El Castillo de Huarmey on the north coast. Tiwanaku: the capital by Lake Titicaca, Bolivia, with colonies in Moquegua. Many museum textiles come from dry south-coast graves in Ica and Nasca. None of these places has a canon ID yet. |\n| Membership confidence | Documented for excavated finds at Wari and Tiwanaku sites. Probable for museum textiles labelled \"Wari\" by style, with no findspot. Contested at the edges: museums label the same hat type \"Wari\", \"Tiwanaku style\" or \"Tiwanaku or Wari\", and Cleveland keeps modern fakes of Wari-style pots. |\n| Leaves out | The makers' own names for their polities: \"Wari\" and \"Tiwanaku\" are modern site names. The weavers, spinners and dyers, none named. Coastal and highland communities who lived alongside or under Wari. The Aymara (COM138), for whom Tiwanaku is ancestral. What the staff-holding figure meant; the Met says the role of such beings \"remains unknown\". |\n",
"| Rights line | Met, Cleveland and Art Institute of Chicago records listed below are CC0 or public domain and may be shown. Many Wari textiles came from burials: show them only with origin and a content note. Never show human remains, mummy bundles or tomb photographs, including El Castillo de Huarmey. Tiwanaku site and Gateway of the Sun: link out to UNESCO. Taquile and Chinchero weavers: link out to their own published pages; photographs only with the weavers' permission. |\n\n**Walk**\n\n| # | Step title | What the learner sees and reads | Nodes | Claim and confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Eight winged figures on a tunic | Two woven panels, stitched together, make a sleeveless, knee-length tunic. Weavers set eight winged feline figures in columns; each holds a staff and carries a patterned cloth. The warp is cotton, the weft camelid hair, and cochineal gives the magenta. | WS-007 (Met 2021.146); TEC092; MAT064; MAT056; MAT043; STY065 | Structure and materials: documented (Met record). What the figures meant: not known; the Met says their role \"remains unknown\". |\n",
"| 2 | Two weavers on one piece | Wari tunics took so much work that sometimes two weavers shared one piece, the Met says. The Art Institute of Chicago counts Wari tunics among the highest thread counts of any handwoven cloth. Figures are squeezed narrower towards the sides, following a design rule. | WS-007; AIC 1956.95; CMA 1949.12; TEC092; STY065 | Labour and thread counts: documented. That the state ran the weaving: argued. The Met says scholars believe production \"was supervised by imperial administrators\"; Jennings's low-power model points instead to rival elite houses. |\n| 3 | A name archaeologists gave to time | \"Middle Horizon\" is a label from John Rowe's Andean chronology, not an ancient name. Rowe named \"horizons\" when one style spread widely, with \"intermediate periods\" between. Dorothy Menzel's 1964 study divided it by pottery epochs; its end is being re-dated (A. P. Rowe 2024). | HOR002; PRD189; HOR001; HOR003; PRD192 | Origin of the term: documented. About 600–1000 CE: probable, the conventional range. The end date: contested. |\n",
"| 4 | The staff god at two capitals | A front-facing figure holds a staff in each hand. At Tiwanaku it is carved at the centre of the Gateway of the Sun's frieze. At Conchopata, near Wari, potters painted it on ceramic vessels. Isbell reads this as \"the initial introduction of Tiahuanaco imagery into Ayacucho\". | POL059; POL058; CIV035; CIV036; BEL038; Conchopata and Tiwanaku (no canon IDs; proposed PLC) | Shared image: documented. Who borrowed from whom: argued. The Met says Ayacucho elites adopted Tiwanaku images; Moseley says the two \"are not borrowing anything from each other\" at their Moquegua border. |\n| 5 | Stone cramps and raised fields at Tiwanaku | Tiwanaku masons cut andesite and sandstone blocks and joined some with I-shaped metal cramps. Around the lake, farmers raised planting beds between water channels, called suka qullu. UNESCO counts almost 50,000 such fields. | POL059; CIV035; COM138; Tiwanaku (UNESCO 2000; no canon ID) | Masonry and fields: documented. Who organised the fields: argued. Kolata (1991) says the state ran them; Erickson (1993) says local communities built and kept them. |\n",
"| 6 | A planned town and a burned brewery | At Pikillacta, near Cusco, Wari builders laid out about 25 hectares of walled compounds. At Cerro Baúl, far south, a brewery held about 1,800 litres of chicha of maize and molle berries. When Wari people left, they feasted, broke 28 fine vessels and burned the buildings. | POL058; OBT075 (kero); Pikillacta and Cerro Baúl (no canon IDs; proposed PLC) | Finds: documented (McEwan; Moseley, Williams and Nash 2005). Read as imperial administration and state feasting: contested (see below). |\n| 7 | Knots wrapped in colour | Wari cord-keepers wrapped coloured thread around pendant cords before knotting. Three Wari khipus are radiocarbon-dated to 780–1024 CE (Cherkinsky and Urton 2014). About fourteen more came from excavations at El Castillo de Huarmey between 2013 and 2016. | OBT139; TEC104; INV005; MKR024 (later Inca keepers); links to F1.5 | Wari khipus as the earliest known securely dated khipus: probable. A Caral cord claim is not securely published (INV005, low). Whether khipus are writing: argued; see F1.5. |\n",
"| 8 | Hats, graves and fakes | Wari and Tiwanaku weavers knotted four-cornered pile hats, which probably marked a wearer's rank. Content note: many such hats and tunics came from graves, and records rarely say which. Looters have dug around El Castillo de Huarmey for decades; Cleveland keeps 20th-century fakes of Wari-style pots. | Met 33.149.101; CMA 1947.291; Met 1994.35.158; CMA 1955.173 (forgeries) | Hats and rank: probable (the Met says \"likely\"). Burial origin: probable for most south-coast pieces, stated on few records. Looting: documented. |\n| 9 | Weavers on Taquile and at Chinchero | Weavers on Taquile, in Lake Titicaca, still weave calendar belts and knit chullo hats. UNESCO proclaimed this practice in 2005 and inscribed it in 2008. Cusco's Centre for Traditional Textiles, founded in 1996, listed ten weaving communities, Chinchero among them, in September 2026. | PRA051; PRA053; COM137; COM138; TEC085 | Living practice: documented, dated to source. We read it as a living afterlife of Andean weaving, not of Wari itself. No direct line from Wari to these weavers is claimed. |\n\n",
"**Members.** In the canon: 1 horizon (HOR002) and its period (PRD189); 1 style (STY065); 2 polities (POL058 Wari, POL059 Tiwanaku) and their 2 civilisation-names (CIV036, CIV035), both under the Andean label (CIV021); 3 object types (OBT075 kero, OBT139 khipu, OBT048 feather cloak, for Wari feathered panels); 4 techniques (TEC092 tapestry weaving, TEC104 khipu construction, TEC122 tie-resist dyeing, TEC109 featherwork); 3 materials (MAT064 camelid fibre, MAT056 cotton, MAT043 cochineal); 1 \"earliest known\" row (INV005 khipu). That is 18 canon nodes. No place is in the canon yet; six are proposed: Huari (Ayacucho), Tiwanaku, Conchopata, Pikillacta, Cerro Baúl and El Castillo de Huarmey. Eight open objects are verified below. The key members are the tapestry tunics and four-cornered hats, because they carry the shared style and the labour argument together.\n\n",
"**Neighbours.** Before it: the Early Horizon of Chavín (HOR001, STY064) and the Early Intermediate Period of Nasca (ARC206) and Moche (ARC207, POL057); Moche-Wari style pieces sit on that seam. After it: the Late Intermediate Period, with Chimor (POL060) and Sicán (ARC209), then the Late Horizon of the Inca (HOR003, POL061, STY066). The Inca state's cloth-makers, the Aqllakuna (MKR023), and its khipu keepers (MKR024) inherit two Middle Horizon crafts. Rival frame: Lumbreras's stage scheme (PRD192) names the same centuries by a Wari empire, not by a style. Parallel elsewhere, with no ranking: the Mississippian Ideological Interaction Sphere (HOR007), another shared iconography whose political meaning is argued.\n\n",
"**Afterlives.** Taquile weaving was proclaimed by UNESCO in 2005 and inscribed in 2008. Men there knit chullo hats and weavers make calendar belts that record the ritual and farming year (PRA051). The Centre for Traditional Textiles of Cusco was founded in 1996 by Andean weavers and supporters. In September 2026 its site listed ten communities, including Chinchero, \"the birthplace of the CTTC\" (PRA053). Raised fields came back in the 1980s: Erickson's project rebuilt fields with Quechua communities at Huatta, Coata and Capachica from 1981 to 1986. By 1989 hundreds of families had adopted them, Erickson reports (1993). Bandy (2005, as summarised on Wikipedia) reports that farmers stopped once projects ended in the 1990s. Remembered: Tiwanaku became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2000. Cleveland's 2012–13 exhibition called Wari \"widely regarded today as ancient Peru's first empire\". The Met showed WS-007 in \"Weaving Abstraction in Ancient and Modern Art\" (5 March–16 June 2024). Sold: Wari cloth has long fed the art market, and so have fakes.\n\n**What is argued.**\n",
"- Was Wari an empire? One reading says yes: an expansive state with planned administrative centres such as Pikillacta and Viracochapampa, and roads (Schreiber, Isbell and McEwan, as summarised by Jennings 2024). Conlee and colleagues (2021) read compounds, a D-shaped temple and non-local burials at Huaca del Loro as Wari colonisation of Nasca. Moseley, Williams and Nash (2005) call Cerro Baúl a Wari colony and read its brewery as state feasting. The other reading says the evidence was fitted to expectation. Jennings (2006) argues the model of administrative centres ruling through reciprocity \"fit[s] our expectations\" more than the data. Jennings (2024) keeps the word \"empire\" but describes low power: rival elite houses and small gatherings, and a heterogeneous horizon of shared ideas, objects and people.\n",
"- Was Tiwanaku a state that ran its fields? Kolata (1991) argues state authority organised raised-field farming, with hierarchical ties between city and countryside; UNESCO calls Tiwanaku \"the capital of a powerful empire\". Erickson (1993) argues farming was organised \"at the local level\": the fields predate the state and continued after it. Wikipedia's summary of later work (Janusek 2004; Bandy 2013) describes a federation of communities or a network of powerful lineages. It notes no defensive architecture, state roads or princely burials.\n- Did the state weave the tunics? The Met's label says scholars believe production \"was supervised by imperial administrators\"; canon STY065 reads the standard designs the same way. Jennings's low-power model would place fine cloth among competing elite houses and gift-giving instead. That extension to cloth is our reading, not his stated claim.\n",
"- Where did the shared image come from, and does sharing mean rule? The Met says Ayacucho elites adopted Tiwanaku images and beliefs; Isbell dates this at Conchopata to Middle Horizon 1A. At Cerro Baúl, Moseley says the two polities \"are not borrowing anything from each other\". Shared iconography alone does not show imperial control; both sides accept this and argue from other evidence.\n\n**Apply** (F1.7 The Americas, read through fibre; khipu shared with F1.5, cloth with F1.18)\n- Maker (M): take the column of figures on WS-007 and redraw one figure on squared paper at three widths: full, half and quarter. Keep every part of the figure; only squeeze it. Then write two sentences on what survives compression and what becomes unreadable.\n- Briefer (B): a client wants a \"Wari-inspired\" textile range. Write a one-page brief. It must name the source objects and their holders, carry the burial content note, and state both readings of Wari. It must route any weaving through a named living community, such as the Cusco centre, with credit and pay terms. It must not claim the weavers descend from Wari.\n\n**Candidate open objects**\n\n",
"1. **Tunic (WS-007).** The Met, 2021.146; 650–1000 CE; Wari; cotton, camelid hair. Licence: public domain (`isPublicDomain: true`; Met Open Access). 1970: pass with caveat. The record gives \"[André Emmerich Gallery, New York, 1960s]; Guido and Nelly di Tella, Buenos Aires, ca. 1960s–2013; Claudia Quentin, New York, 2013–2021\". It left Peru before 1970 on the record, but the Met brackets the 1960s step, marking it unconfirmed. Findspot not stated. [Record](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/675980)\n2. **Tunic.** Art Institute of Chicago, 1956.95; 600–800 CE; Wari; origin given as Nazca Valley. Licence: public domain (`is_public_domain: true`); data CC0, description field CC BY. 1970: pass. \"Pablo Soldi, Ica, Peru, by May 21, 1956 … sold to the Art Institute of Chicago, 1956\", so it left Peru in 1956. Content note: probably from a south-coast grave; the record does not say. [Record](https://www.artic.edu/artworks/2091)\n",
"3. **Tunic with Profile Animal and Human Heads.** Cleveland Museum of Art, 1949.12; c. 700–1100; Wari, south coast; camelid fibre and cotton tapestry. Licence: CC0. 1970: pass on accession year (1949). The record gives only \"Gift of John Wise\", with no earlier history. [Record](https://clevelandart.org/art/1949.12)\n4. **Tapestry Square Panel.** Cleveland Museum of Art, 1957.495; c. 700–1100; Wari, south coast; interlocked tapestry, wool and cotton. Licence: CC0. 1970: pass on accession year (1957). The record gives only \"Gift of John Wise in memory of Leonard C. Hanna Jr.\" [Record](https://clevelandart.org/art/1957.495)\n5. **Four-Cornered Hat.** The Met, 33.149.101; 7th–9th century; Wari; camelid hair, cotton. Licence: public domain. 1970: pass; \"Gift of George D. Pratt, 1933\". Earlier history and findspot not stated. [Record](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/308121)\n",
"6. **Four-Cornered Hat.** Cleveland Museum of Art, 1947.291; c. 700–1100; labelled \"Wari Culture, Tiwanaku Style\"; needle network with cut pile. Licence: CC0. 1970: pass on accession year (1947); \"Gift of John Wise\", no earlier history. Useful for step 8 as a label case. [Record](https://clevelandart.org/art/1947.291)\n7. **Four-Cornered Hat.** The Met, 1994.35.158; 500–900 CE; labelled \"Tiwanaku or Wari\"; camelid fibre. Licence: public domain. 1970: unknown. \"Bequest of Arthur M. Bullowa, 1993\", and the provenance field is empty. Hold for display until its history before 1970 is found; use as a link-out case on attribution. [Record](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/316978)\n8. **Kero (beaker).** The Met, 1978.412.214; 500–1000 CE; Tiwanaku; wood. Licence: public domain. 1970: pass on the credit line, \"Purchase, Nelson A. Rockefeller Gift, 1968\". Earlier history and findspot not stated. [Record](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/310659)\n\n",
"Not listed but checked: Cleveland 1955.173, \"Wari-style Vessels\", 20th-century forgeries, CC0, gift of William R. Carlisle, 1955 ([record](https://clevelandart.org/art/1955.173)). No open record of a Wari khipu was found at the Met, Cleveland or AIC. For step 7, link out to Dumbarton Oaks and the Museo de Arte de Lima, or use a drawing of our own of a wrapped pendant cord.\n\n**Sources**\n- [Tunic, Wari, 2021.146 (Met collection record)](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/675980) and [Met API record 675980](https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/675980)\n- [Four-Cornered Hat, 1994.35.158 (Met)](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/316978); [Met API 308121](https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/308121); [Met API 310659](https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/310659)\n- [Art Institute of Chicago API, artwork 2091 (Tunic, 1956.95)](https://api.artic.edu/api/v1/artworks/2091)\n",
"- [Cleveland Museum of Art Open Access API, search \"Wari\"](https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/?q=Wari&cc0=1); records [1949.12](https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/1949.12), [1957.495](https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/1957.495), [1947.291](https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/1947.291)\n- [Wari: Lords of the Ancient Andes (Cleveland Museum of Art, 2012–13)](https://www.clevelandart.org/exhibitions/wari-lords-ancient-andes)\n- [José Ochatoma Paravicino, \"Wari Imperial Art\", Heilbrunn Timeline, the Met (2025)](https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/wari-imperial-art)\n- [Justin Jennings, \"Understanding Middle Horizon Peru: Hermeneutic Spirals, Interpretative Traditions, and Wari Administrative Centers\", Latin American Antiquity 17 (2006)](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/latin-american-antiquity/article/abs/understanding-middle-horizon-peru-hermeneutic-spirals-interpretative-traditions-and-wari-administrative-centers/5D03EAC0346ABB3B29C1E45F71A6F7CB)\n",
"- [Justin Jennings, \"Wari: Imperialism, Low Power, and Globalization in the Middle Horizon Central Andes\", Journal of Archaeological Research (2024)](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10814-024-09199-1)\n- [Christina A. Conlee and others, \"Early imperialism in the Andes: Wari colonisation of Nasca\", Antiquity 95 (2021)](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/antiquity/article/early-imperialism-in-the-andes-wari-colonisation-of-nasca/26211E836214FF4A39194FAF8EEAC360)\n- [\"Burning down the brewery\" (EurekAlert, 14 November 2005, on Moseley, Williams and Nash in PNAS)](https://www.eurekalert.org/news-releases/512680)\n- [Pikillaqta (Wikipedia; cites McEwan 1996, 2005)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikillaqta)\n- [Wari culture (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wari_culture)\n- [Gary Urton, \"From Middle Horizon cordkeeping to the rise of Inka khipus in the central Andes\", Antiquity 88 (2014)](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/antiquity/article/abs/from-middle-horizon-cordkeeping-to-the-rise-of-inka-khipus-in-the-central-andes/04964C31803194A5EA0F1565617F00C9)\n",
"- [Alexander Cherkinsky and Gary Urton, \"Radiocarbon chronology of Andean khipus\", Open Journal of Archaeometry (2014)](https://journals.pagepress.net/arc/article/view/arc.2014.5260)\n- [Jeffrey Splitstoser and Milosz Giersz, \"Wari-Style Khipus from El Castillo de Huarmey\" (tDAR; SAA 81st Annual Meeting)](https://core.tdar.org/document/431807/wari-style-khipus-from-el-castillo-de-huarmey)\n- [Written in Knots: Undeciphered Accounts of Andean Life (Dumbarton Oaks, 2019)](https://www.doaks.org/visit/museum/exhibitions/past/written-in-knots)\n- [Wari Khipus (MALI, Google Arts & Culture; story by Jeffrey Splitstoser)](https://artsandculture.google.com/story/wari-khipus-mali-museo-de-arte-de-lima/jAXhk_JrDHGq4w?hl=en)\n- [El Castillo de Huarmey (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castillo_de_Huarmey)\n- [Staff God and Profile Attendants, Conchopata (Southern Andean Iconographic Series, UCLA; William H. Isbell)](https://dig.ucla.edu/sais/node/5264)\n- [Staff God (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staff_God)\n",
"- [Alan L. Kolata, \"The Technology and Organization of Agricultural Production in the Tiwanaku State\", Latin American Antiquity 2 (1991)](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/latin-american-antiquity/article/abs/technology-and-organization-of-agricultural-production-in-the-tiwanaku-state/EA405A0051AB28ACD20EAE5AC27F455A)\n- [Clark L. Erickson, \"The Social Organization of Prehispanic Raised Field Agriculture in the Lake Titicaca Basin\", Research in Economic Anthropology, Supplement 7 (1993)](https://anthropology.sas.upenn.edu/sites/default/files/page/EricksonSocialOrganizationRaisedFieldAgricultureLakeTiticaca1999b.pdf)\n- [Tiwanaku polity (Wikipedia; summarises Janusek 2004, Bandy 2005 and 2013)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiwanaku_polity)\n- [Tiwanaku: Spiritual and Political Centre of the Tiwanaku Culture (UNESCO World Heritage List, 2000)](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/567/)\n- [Ann Pollard Rowe, \"Redefining the End of the Middle Horizon: Part 1\", Ñawpa Pacha 44 (2024)](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00776297.2024.2370682)\n- [Pre-Inca cultures (Wikipedia; Rowe and Lanning chronology chart)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pre-Inca_cultures)\n",
"- [Taquile and its textile art (UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage)](https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/taquile-and-its-textile-art-00166)\n- [Centro de Textiles Tradicionales del Cusco](https://www.textilescusco.org/) and [its communities page](https://www.textilescusco.org/our-communities)\n"
