"## Grouping page: The waqf (pious endowment)\n\nA founder gave property away forever, and its rents paid for buildings, lamps, books and their repair for centuries.\n\n**Card**\n\n| Part | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Kind | Institution: a commissioning regime in Islamic law (INS044). A founder (*wāqif*) dedicates property in perpetuity; its income pays for a named charitable purpose |\n| Defined by (who, when) | Muslim jurists, from hadith about perpetual charity; the full legal form probably dates from the 9th century (Wikipedia, \"Waqf\"). Each foundation is defined by its own deed (*waqfiyya*, Ottoman *vakfiye*), validated by a qadi and two witnesses (Isoldi 2025) |\n| Span | Earliest dated deed 876 CE (Wikipedia, \"Waqf\"); living today, run in most Muslim-majority states by a ministry or directorate of awqaf (Britannica) |\n| Places | Cairo, Damascus, Jerusalem, Istanbul, Edirne, Fez, Algiers, Mecca and Medina, and on across West Asia, North Africa and South Asia (canon INS044 regions WA-MES; AF-EGY; WA-ANA; AS-SAS) |\n",
"| Membership confidence | High for buildings whose deed survives (Sultan Hasan, Khawand Baraka's madrasa, Hürrem Sultan's Jerusalem foundation). Medium for loose objects: a lamp or stand counts as endowed when its own inscription says \"dedicated\" or \"waqf\". Low for founders known only from later chronicles (Fatima al-Fihri) |\n| Leaves out | The glassmakers, potters, masons and scribes, almost all unnamed; the peasants and tenants whose rents paid for everything; enslaved people inside founders' households (mamluks were bought as enslaved boys; Hürrem Sultan was taken captive; Sinan was taken in the devshirme levy). Content note where they appear |\n| Rights line | Met objects marked public domain (CC0) and Cleveland CC0 records may be shown with their images. Deeds held in Istanbul and Cairo are link-out only. Buildings via Wikimedia Commons, licence checked per file. Qur'an leaves and the Qur'an stand carry the canon's \"sacred\" flag: shown only as the project's sacred-material decision allows, never cropped through the text. The Aydakin lamp was made for a tomb: content note |\n\n**Walk**\n\n| # | Step title | What the learner sees and reads | Nodes | Claim and confidence |\n",
"|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | The deed comes first | A bound endowment charter, dated AH 964 / 1556–57, names Hürrem Sultan's foundation in Jerusalem. It lists a mosque, a madrasa and a soup kitchen, and the shops, mills and estates whose income keeps them running. The learner reads the deed as the brief: who pays, for what, forever. | INS044; PER317; deed: Museum of Turkish and Islamic Arts, Istanbul, inv. 2192 (link-out) | Documented (MWNF record) |\n| 2 | A city block paid for by rents | The Süleymaniye in Istanbul was founded in 1550 and inaugurated in 1557, by its inscription. Four madrasas, a hospital, a soup kitchen, a guesthouse, a bath and rent-paying shops surrounded the mosque. Iznik potters tiled it: the earliest known Iznik tiles with tomato-red and emerald green under the glaze. | PER316 (patron); PER319 (Sinan, taken in the devshirme, a forced levy); STY109; MKR057; PLC305; INS042 | Documented (Wikipedia, \"Süleymaniye Mosque\", citing the inscription and deed) |\n",
"| 3 | The budget line for light | Mamluk deeds paid for maintenance before anything else, then salaries. Only then came food, water, lamps, oil, incense, carpets, brooms and rags (Haarmann 1980). Sultan Hasan's deed, which partly survives, provided for over 500 students, 200 schoolboys and 340 staff. | PER308; POL042; STY104 | Documented (Haarmann; Smarthistory) |\n| 4 | A lamp that says \"dedicated\" | Glassmakers in Egypt or Syria, unnamed, blew, enamelled and gilded this lamp around 1325. Its inscription reads \"This is what was dedicated by… Ahmad al-Mihmandar\" for the madrasa he built in Cairo in 1324–25. The Met thinks it was probably one of several. | OBT024; TEC049; STY104; Met 91.1.1534; Met 17.190.985 (lamp for the tomb of Amir Aydakin, d. 1285) | Documented (inscription); probable (\"one of several\") |\n",
"| 5 | Books and furniture in the deed | Hasan ibn Sulaiman al-Isfahani carved this teak Qur'an stand in 1360. Its inscription records it as an endowment to a madrasa. A 14th-century Cairo Qur'an leaf in Cleveland carries a waqf note, marking the book as the foundation's property. | OBT183; GAP024; BEL017; Met 10.218; Cleveland 2013.102 | Documented (inscriptions as the museums read them) |\n| 6 | Women who endowed | Khawand Baraka endowed her madrasa in Cairo in 1369–70; its deed lists rents and farmland in Egypt and Syria. Hürrem Sultan funded foundations in Istanbul, Jerusalem, Mecca and Edirne (canon PER317). Fatima al-Fihri's founding of the Qarawiyyin in Fez in 859 is argued: see below. | PER317; PER318; PER302; PLC023 (Kairouan, her family's city in the tradition) | Documented (Baraka, Hürrem); contested (Fatima al-Fihri) |\n",
"| 7 | Repair written into the rules | Ottoman deeds say to spend income \"…first [on] repair and maintenance…\" (*evvela tamir ve termimine*). Süleyman's deed asks for repairs without delay; Mehmed II's deed staffs repair workers and a store of materials (Akar 2021). The Süleymaniye was repaired after the fire of 1660 and the earthquake of 1766. | INS044; PER316 | Documented (Akar; Wikipedia, \"Süleymaniye Mosque\") |\n| 8 | States take the endowments | In Algeria, 1844 French legislation let \"inalienable\" habous property be sold whenever a European was party. From 1881 a conservation committee inside Egypt's waqf ministry restored, and rebuilt, Mamluk mosques. Decree-law 180 of 14 September 1952 abolished Egypt's family endowments. | OCC151 (neighbour: Ottoman state control of antiquities); POL042 | Documented (dates); contested (what the takeovers were for) |\n",
"| 9 | The directorate that still repairs | Turkey's General Directorate of Foundations restored the Süleymaniye in 2007–2010. About 200 people stripped out 1960s cement render and relaid lime-based *horasan* mortar. The mosque reopened for prayer in November 2010 (Hürriyet; Dünya). | INS044; PER319 | Documented, dated to 2010 press |\n\n",
"**Members.** 1 institution (INS044), with 10 people, 4 places and makers, 5 object types and 3 styles or techniques lit by the walk. People: Hürrem Sultan (PER317), Suleiman I (PER316), Mimar Sinan (PER319), Sultan Hasan (PER308), Fatima al-Fihri (PER302, contested), and, without canon IDs yet, Khawand Baraka, Ahmad al-Mihmandar, Amir Aydakin, Sultan Barquq and the maker Hasan ibn Sulaiman al-Isfahani. Places and makers: Iznik and its potteries (PLC305, MKR057), the court studio (INS042), Kairouan (PLC023); Mihrimah Sultan (PER318) commissioned two Sinan mosques, but our sources did not open her deeds; the Süleymaniye, Sultan Hasan complex and Qarawiyyin need place IDs. Object types: mosque lamp (OBT024), Qur'an stand (OBT183), illuminated Qur'an (GAP024), tile, carpet (OBT184 prayer rug as the nearest). Styles and techniques: Mamluk style (STY104), Ottoman classical (STY109), enamelled and gilded glass (TEC049). Polity: Mamluk Sultanate (POL042). Canon links not yet made: PER231 Jahanara Begum and PER301 Zubaidah bint Ja'far funded caravanserais and pilgrim wells, but our sources did not say whether through waqf.\n\n",
"**Neighbours.** It defined itself against inheritance and the ruler's treasury: property made waqf could not be sold, inherited or seized, and founders used it partly to protect wealth from confiscation (Hesperis-Tamuda 2020, citing Saidouni). Wikipedia (\"Waqf\") notes its close resemblance to the English trust. Parallel groupings elsewhere: the Christian chantry, monastic and hospital foundations, whose English property the crown seized in 1536–41 (OCC059), a mirror of the takeovers in step 8. The Khmer king Jayavarman VII's temples, hospitals and rest houses are listed in inscriptions (PER273). Hindu temple endowments under the Chola kings (POL192; Thanjavur temple, 1010) are a third neighbour; their endowment records still need a source here. Isoldi (2025) notes that scholars link the waqf's development to Byzantine *piae causae* and the Jewish *heqdesh*. We read all of these as parallels of form, not of descent, unless a source shows contact.\n\n",
"**Afterlives.** Waqf is living law, run in most Muslim-majority states by a ministry (Britannica). In 2007–2010 Turkey's General Directorate of Foundations (Vakıflar Genel Müdürlüğü) spent 21 million lira on the Süleymaniye. Its regional office said earlier restorations ran in 1847–49 and 1959–60, and that the 1960s cement had trapped damp and salts (Hürriyet, 9 November 2010). The mosque reopened for prayer at Eid al-Adha in November 2010 (Dünya, 16 November 2010). In Fez the Qarawiyyin library was restored from 2012 by the architect Aziza Chaouni, approached by Morocco's Culture Ministry, and reopened in 2016 (AP, 19 April 2016). The lamps became collectors' prizes. In the late 1800s the Parisian craftsman Philippe-Joseph Brocard revived Mamluk enamelling and made many lamp copies (Cleveland 1981.10).\n\n**What is argued.**\n",
"- *Who founded the Qarawiyyin?* One reading: Fatima al-Fihri, a woman from Kairouan, spent her inheritance on the mosque from 859; this is Ibn Abi Zar's account (d. 1310–20), repeated by UNESCO and Guinness. The other: Roger Le Tourneau (1949) and Jonathan Bloom find the two-sisters, two-mosques story too neat. Chafik Benchekroun (2011) reads a Kufic inscription naming Dawud ibn Idris, dated 877, as the mosque's own foundation text, and calls Fatima possibly legendary (Wikipedia, \"Fatima al-Fihriya\").\n- *Colonial takeover: plan or muddle?* The classic account (Ruedy 1967; Powers 1989) has France set out to dismantle habous in Algeria, climaxing in the 1844 law. Saidouni and Saidouni (2009, 2023) find two French camps, one for seizure and one for gradual reform, until the 1873 seizure (Hesperis-Tamuda 2020; Rivisteweb abstract).\n- *Why did Egypt abolish family waqf in 1952?* The official reason was misuse and bad management by trustees. Ibrahim al-Bayoumi Ghanem (2021) argues the decree-law, issued a week after the land-reform law, mainly served land reform and weakened the old landowning class (ida2at; Persée 1953).\n",
"- *Conservation or reinvention?* The committee inside Egypt's waqf ministry (1881–1936 under Awqaf) saved Mamluk buildings. It also rebuilt minarets, domes and minbars to its own idea of them; Bakhoum (2021) asks whether this was \"conservation\" or \"reinvention\".\n\n**Apply** (F1.17 Who commissions; also used in F1.10 The Islamic world as a network)\n- **M (Maker):** Design one object for a public building that must still work in 200 years: a lamp, a bench or a sign. Write its inscription, what it is made of, and the one repair it will need most. Say who is paid to do that repair.\n- **B (Briefer):** Write a one-page \"deed\" for a commission. List the income that pays for it, the order in which money is spent (repair first, as in the Ottoman deeds), the staff, and what happens if the purpose ends. Then name one way a later state or owner could override it.\n\n**Candidate open objects**\n\n| # | Holder and ID | What and date | Licence (as holder states) | 1970 status | Record |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| 1 | The Met, 91.1.1534 | Mosque lamp of Amir Ahmad al-Mihmandar, glass, blown, enamelled and gilded; ca. 1325 (range 1300–1350) | Public domain (isPublicDomain: true; Open Access CC0) | Pass: Edward C. Moore bequest, 1891. How it left Cairo is not recorded | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/444713 |\n| 2 | The Met, 17.190.985 | Mosque lamp for the mausoleum of Amir Aydakin al-'Ala'i al-Bunduqdar; shortly after 1285 (range 1260–1310) | Public domain (CC0) | Pass: gift of J. Pierpont Morgan, 1917. Earlier history not recorded. Funerary setting: content note | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/447000 |\n| 3 | The Met, 17.190.989 | Mosque lamp of Sultan Barquq; ca. 1382–99 | Public domain (CC0) | Pass: gift of J. Pierpont Morgan, 1917. Earlier history not recorded | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/447004 |\n| 4 | The Met, 10.218 | Stand for a Qur'an manuscript, teak, carved, painted and inlaid, by Hasan ibn Sulaiman al-Isfahani; dated 761 AH / 1360 CE | Public domain (CC0) | Pass: Rogers Fund, 1910. Canon flag \"sacred\" | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/446151 |\n",
"| 5 | Cleveland Museum of Art, 2013.102 | Leaf from a Qur'an, Cairo, Mamluk; 1300s; 13 lines of muhaqqaq script with a waqf inscription | CC0 | Not shown: earliest recorded owner is a Christie's sale, 27–28 June 2006. No pre-1970 history. Canon flag \"sacred\" | https://clevelandart.org/art/2013.102 |\n| 6 | Cleveland Museum of Art, 1944.236 | Mosque lamp, fritware with underglaze painting, Iznik; 1585–95 | CC0 | Pass: Heeramaneck Galleries, sold to Cleveland 1944 | https://clevelandart.org/art/1944.236 |\n| 7 | Cleveland Museum of Art, 1981.10 | Mosque lamp, France, enamelled and gilded glass; late 1800s; from Philippe-Joseph Brocard's collection, possibly made by him | CC0 | Chain from the 1800s: Brocard, Demotte, Ispenian, Spink, sold to Cleveland 1981. A European copy, not a looting case | https://clevelandart.org/art/1981.10 |\n\nNo endowment deed is available under an open licence among the records checked: Hürrem Sultan's charter is link-out only (Museum of Turkish and Islamic Arts, inv. 2192, via MWNF). For the deed step, use the link-out plus a drawing of our own of the deed's parts: founder, property, income, order of spending, staff, trustee, witnesses.\n\n**Sources**\n",
